    "title": "You Already Play These",
    "summary": "The open C chord dissected into three overlapping triads, plus open A and open D on the same strings — and the one open chord that isn't a triad at all.",
    "tags": ["triads", "chords"],
    "readingTimeMin": 3,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Open C — " },
        { "text": "x 3 2 0 1 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", the chord from your first month — isn't one triad. It's three of them, stacked inside the same five fingers, on three overlapping string sets, in all three inversions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": [
        [{ "text": "Strings" }],
        [{ "text": "Frets" }],
        [{ "text": "Notes" }],
        [{ "text": "Reading" }]
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          [{ "text": "5-4-3" }],
          [{ "text": "3 2 0", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "C E G", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "root position, set 3-4-5" }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "4-3-2" }],
          [{ "text": "2 0 1", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "E G C", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "first inversion, set 2-3-4" }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "3-2-1" }],
          [{ "text": "0 1 0", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "G C E", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "second inversion, set 1-2-3" }]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "None of this is new material — it's a new way of reading chords you've had under your fingers for years. Every reading above uses the same vocabulary from earlier in this chapter: root position, first inversion, second inversion, named the same way no matter which strings they land on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The middle reading — " },
        { "text": "2 0 1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "E G C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — is the " },
        { "text": "C/E", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " grip from the inversion lesson: the same first inversion, read straight out of a chord you already knew by heart before you knew what it was."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Open A does the same trick with no overlap to explain. " },
        { "text": "x 0 2 2 2 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — its top three strings, " },
        { "text": "2 2 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — are " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " " },
        { "text": "E", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ": an A major triad in root position on strings 1-2-3, the same shape as C's root position, moved to a new root."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "triad-shape",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "strings": "1-2-3",
        "inversion": "root",
        "caption": "Open A's top three strings: A major, root position."
      }
    },
    {
